# Analysis of W v. The Registrar of Marriages [2013] HKCFA 39

## 1. Summary of Facts

The appellant, W, is a post-operative male-to-female transsexual person residing in Hong Kong. She was registered as male at birth but from an early age perceived herself as female. She was diagnosed with gender identity disorder and underwent psychiatric assessment and hormonal treatment between 2005 and 2008. In January 2007, she had an orchidectomy performed in Thailand, and in 2008, she underwent full sex reassignment surgery (SRS) at Hospital Authority facilities in Hong Kong, involving removal of her penis and construction of an artificial vagina. She subsequently obtained a Hospital Authority certificate confirming her gender change to female, and was issued a new identity card and passport reflecting her female gender.

W wished to marry her male partner. On 17 November 2008, her solicitors wrote to the Registrar of Marriages seeking confirmation that she could marry. The Registrar refused, stating that under section 40 of the Marriage Ordinance (Cap 181), marriage is "the voluntary union for life of one man and one woman" and that "the biological sexual constitution of an individual is fixed at birth and cannot be changed, either by the natural development of organs of the opposite sex, or by medical or surgical means." The Registrar took the position that only an individual's sex at birth counts for marriage purposes, and any operative intervention is ignored.

W brought judicial review proceedings challenging this decision. Her challenge failed at first instance before Andrew Cheung J and in the Court of Appeal. Leave to appeal to the Court of Final Appeal was granted on the basis of questions of great general or public importance concerning: (1) whether the words "woman" and "female" in the Marriage Ordinance include a post-operative male-to-female transsexual; and (2) if not, whether those provisions are unconstitutional having regard to the right to marry under Article 37 of the Basic Law and/or Article 19(2) of the Hong Kong Bill of Rights, and/or the right to privacy under Article 14 of the Bill of Rights.

## 2. Summary of Arguments

**The Appellant's Arguments (W):**

Lord Pannick QC, appearing for W, argued that she ought in law to count as a woman for the purposes of marriage. The contention was that she is for legal purposes a woman and entitled to marry a person of the opposite sex. It was made clear that it was no part of the appellant's case that same-sex marriage should be permitted.

On statutory construction, W argued that the words "woman" and "female" in the Marriage Ordinance should be given their ordinary meaning, which in contemporary Hong Kong should include a post-operative male-to-female transsexual person. Alternatively, even if the statutory provisions, properly construed, excluded her, they were unconstitutional.

On the constitutional question, W argued that the right to marry under Article 37 of the Basic Law and Article 19(2) of the Bill of Rights was engaged. She submitted that the Corbett criteria (biological factors fixed at birth) were incomplete and failed to give proper effect to the constitutional right to marry. She argued that the Court should adopt an updated interpretation of the constitutional provisions as living instruments, taking account of medical advances and changed societal attitudes. She further argued that the statutory provisions, construed as excluding her, impaired the very essence of her right to marry, since she had no possibility of marrying a woman (given that she had undergone irreversible surgery and lived as a woman). She also relied on the right to privacy under Article 14 of the Bill of Rights in support of her right to marry argument.

W submitted that the Court should either strike down the offending provisions or apply an appropriate remedial interpretation to render them constitutional.

**The Respondent's Arguments (The Registrar of Marriages):**

Ms Monica Carss-Frisk QC, appearing for the Registrar, argued that the statutory provisions should be construed according to their ordinary meaning, which referred to biological sex fixed at birth. She submitted that the legislative intent behind section 20(1)(d) of the Matrimonial Causes Ordinance was to adopt the Corbett approach, and that the Hong Kong legislature had consciously adopted the English Nullity of Marriage Act 1971 provision.

On the constitutional question, the Registrar argued that at the times when the relevant constitutional instruments were promulgated (the ECHR in 1950, the ICCPR in 1966, the Joint Declaration in 1984, and the Basic Law in 1990), the framers should be assumed to have adopted a traditional approach to marriage and to who qualifies as a "woman" for the purposes of the right to marry, along lines similar to Corbett. There was no evidence of a social consensus in Hong Kong or an international consensus among ICCPR State Parties in favour of permitting a transsexual woman like W to marry a man.

The Registrar further argued that the repercussions of legally recognizing the acquired gender of someone like W for the purposes of marriage were so far-reaching and complex that the Court should refrain from intervening in a piecemeal fashion and should instead leave any changes to be made systematically by the legislature. The Registrar relied on the approach of the House of Lords in Bellinger v Bellinger, where their Lordships declined to adopt a construction differing from Corbett on the basis that departing from the established law would involve such a major change with far-reaching ramifications that it should be left to the legislature.

The Registrar also argued that there was no consensus in Hong Kong society in favour of permitting such marriages, and that the Court should be reluctant to declare the provisions unconstitutional in the absence of such consensus.

## 3. Legal Principles (Ratio Decidendi)

The Court of Final Appeal, by a majority (Chan PJ dissenting), allowed the appeal. The joint judgment of Chief Justice Ma and Mr Justice Ribeiro PJ contains the principal reasoning, with Mr Justice Bokhary NPJ delivering a concurring judgment and Lord Hoffmann NPJ agreeing with the joint judgment.

**Statutory Construction:**

The Court held that, as a matter of statutory construction, the Registrar did not misconstrue section 40 of the Marriage Ordinance or section 20(1)(d) of the Matrimonial Causes Ordinance. The statutory intention behind the enactment of section 20(1)(d) was to reproduce in Hong Kong's statute book section 1(c) of the Nullity of Marriage Act 1971 (later re-enacted as section 11(c) of the Matrimonial Causes Act 1973), and thereby to import into Hong Kong the endorsement which the United Kingdom Parliament gave to the Corbett criteria for determining who was "a woman" for the purposes of marriage. Since section 40 of the Marriage Ordinance covered materially the same ground, the Registrar's approach was in accordance with the true construction of both provisions.

The Court rejected the argument based on "ordinary meaning," holding that the reference to "ordinary meaning" must not obscure the crucial importance of context and purpose when construing the relevant provisions. One is not concerned with asking whether a post-operative transsexual woman is "a woman" in some abstract or general sense, but whether she is "a woman" for the purposes of the law of marriage and so has capacity to marry a man. The Court emphasized that it is perfectly possible that, as a matter of law, someone in W's position may qualify as a woman for some, but not all purposes.

**Constitutional Principles:**

The Court held that the Corbett criteria, which underlie the construction of section 20(1)(d) of the Matrimonial Causes Ordinance and section 40 of the Marriage Ordinance, are incomplete and unconstitutional. The Court's reasoning may be summarized as follows:

*The Nature of the Constitutional Right to Marry:*

The Court held that while the right to marry is necessarily subject to legal rules regulating its exercise, such rules must be consistent with the constitutional right and must not operate to impair the very essence of that right. The legal rules governing the exercise of the right to marry must be compatible with Article 37 of the Basic Law and Article 19(2) of the Bill of Rights and must not impair the essence of the right.

*The Inadequacy of the Corbett Criteria:*

The Court held that the Corbett criteria are incomplete in that they are limited to a person's biological features existing at the time of birth and treated as immutable. They ignore the psychological and social elements of a person's sexual identity and ignore any sex reassignment treatment that has occurred. As such, they do not permit a full and appropriate assessment of the sexual identity of a person to be made for the purposes of determining whether he or she has the right to marry. In adopting such restrictive criteria, the provisions are inconsistent with and fail to give proper effect to the constitutional right to marry. They are therefore unconstitutional.

The Court emphasized that the concept of marriage adopted in Corbett was derived from the classic description of a Christian marriage in Hyde v Hyde with an emphasis on procreative sexual intercourse being an essential purpose of the matrimonial union. However, the Court held that in present-day multi-cultural Hong Kong, procreation is no longer (if it ever was) regarded as essential to marriage. There is no justification for regarding the ability to engage in procreative sexual intercourse as a sine qua non of marriage and thus as the premise for deducing purely biological criteria for ascertaining a person's sex for marriage purposes.

The Court further held that it is not easy to see any justification for confining the criteria for deciding who counts as "a woman" for marriage purposes to biological criteria fixed at birth, and ignoring the psychological, post-operative and social dimensions of the transsexual person's sexual identity viewed at the time of the proposed marriage. Such a selective choice of criteria is particularly hard to justify in the light of significant medical advances in the treatment of transsexualism and important changes in the understanding of and social attitudes towards transsexual persons which have occurred over the last 40 odd years.

*The Importance of Psychological and Social Factors:*

The Court held that the importance of the psychological and social dimensions of a transsexual person's sexual identity is now far better understood than in Corbett's time. The Court noted that Ormrod J's description of the transsexual woman in Corbett as representing a "pastiche of femininity" and his likening of her to a "female impersonator" suggested an element of artifice and betrayed a failure to recognize the fundamental importance and potency of the individual's psychological compulsion as a determinant of her sexual identity.

The Court held that the fact that transsexual persons are willing to endure a long and painful ordeal to acquire a body which conforms as far as possible with their self-perception and to struggle for social recognition in their acquired gender is clear evidence of the fundamental importance of the psychological factor as a determinant of their sexual identity. For the law to exclude that factor as a criterion is quite unjustifiable.

*The Appropriate Criteria:*

The Court held that in addressing the question whether an individual like W qualifies as "a woman" so as to be entitled to marry a man, the Court ought in principle to consider all the circumstances – biological, psychological and social – relevant to assessing that individual's sexual identity at the time of the proposed marriage. The Court can see no good reason for the Court to adopt criteria which are fixed at the time of the relevant person's birth and regarded as immutable. That is to adopt a blinkered view, looking only at circumstances existing at a time when the psychological element – which is so important to the sexual identity of transsexuals – was not manifest, and when the surgical and social transformation of the individual had not yet taken place. It is contrary to principle that the Court, in making the important determination of whether a transsexual person has in law the right to marry, should be prevented from taking account of all the available evidence.

*Impairing the Very Essence of the Right:*

The Court further held that the statutory provisions, construed as endorsing the Corbett criteria, operate to prevent W from marrying at all. In the light of the irreversible surgery which she has undergone to eliminate the original male genital and gonadal organs, and in the light of her implacable rejection of her male sexual identity, there is no question of her enjoying in any meaningful sense the right to marry by being able to marry a woman. The Court held that it is artificial to assert that post-operative transsexuals have not been deprived of the right to marry as, according to law, they remain able to marry a person of their former opposite sex. W lives as a woman, is in a relationship with a man and would only wish to marry a man. She has no possibility of doing so. She may therefore claim that the very essence of her right to marry has been infringed. The provisions are therefore unconstitutional for this additional reason.

*The Remedial Interpretation:*

The Court held that it is necessary in principle that a remedial interpretation should be given to section 20(1)(d) of the Matrimonial Causes Ordinance and section 40 of the Marriage Ordinance. It is a remedial interpretation which requires the references to "woman" and "female" to be read as capable of accommodating post-operative male-to-female transsexual persons for marriage purposes and as allowing account to be taken of the full range of criteria for assessing sexual identity, viewed at the date of the marriage or proposed marriage.

The Court held that a transsexual in W's situation, that is, one who has gone through full SRS, should in principle be granted a declaration that, consistently with Article 37 of the Basic Law and Article 19(2) of the Bill of Rights, she is in law entitled to be included as "a woman" within the meaning of section 40 of the Marriage Ordinance and section 20(1)(d) of the Matrimonial Causes Ordinance and therefore eligible to marry a man. The Court would not seek to lay down a rule that only those who have had full gender reassignment surgery involving both excising and reconstructive genital surgery qualify. The Court left open the question whether transsexual persons who have undergone less extensive treatment might also qualify.

The Court held that a transsexual person who has been issued with a certificate that his or her gender has been changed on the basis that the original genital organs have been removed and some form of the genital organs of the opposite sex have been constructed, ought in any event to qualify as a person entitled to marry in his or her acquired gender.

*The Rejection of the Consensus Argument:*

The Court rejected the Registrar's argument that the Court should be reluctant to declare the provisions unconstitutional unless persuaded that there is a general consensus among the people of Hong Kong in favour of permitting such individuals to marry in their acquired gender. The Court held that reliance on the absence of a majority consensus as a reason for rejecting a minority's claim is inimical in principle to fundamental rights. The Court cited with approval the extra-judicial observation of Murray CJ of Ireland that the use of consensus as an interpretive tool is inherently problematic because the very application of a doctrine of consensus by a court required to adjudicate on fundamental rights begs important questions of legitimacy. How can resort to the will of the majority dictate the decisions of a court whose role is to interpret universal and indivisible human rights, especially minority rights?

*The Living Instrument Doctrine:*

The Court accepted that the Basic Law and the Bill of Rights are living instruments intended to meet changing needs and circumstances. However, the Court emphasized that it is one thing to have regard to such changes as a basis for accepting a more generous interpretation of a fundamental right and quite another to point to the absence of a majority consensus as a reason for denying recognition of minority rights.

*The Relief:*

The Court directed that the parties be at liberty to lodge within 21 days from the date of the judgment submissions in writing in respect of the appropriate Orders to be made in the light of the Court's judgment and also submissions on costs, with liberty to file written submissions in reply, if any, within 14 days thereafter.

Subject to any modifications which the Court may consider warranted in the light of such submissions and subject to the question of costs, the Court proposed the following Orders:

(a) That the appeal be allowed;

(b) That a Declaration be granted that, consistently with Article 37 of the Basic Law and Article 19(2) of the Hong Kong Bill of Rights, section 20(1)(d) of the Matrimonial Causes Ordinance and section 40 of the Marriage Ordinance must be read and given effect so as to include within the meaning of the words "woman" and "female" a post-operative male-to-female transsexual person whose gender has been certified by an appropriate medical authority to have changed as a result of sex reassignment surgery;

(c) That a Declaration be granted that the appellant is in law entitled to be included as "a woman" within the meaning of section 20(1)(d) of the Matrimonial Causes Ordinance and section 40 of the Marriage Ordinance and is accordingly eligible to marry a man;

(d) That the Declarations in paragraphs (b) and (c) shall not come into effect until the expiry of 12 months from the date of this Order.

**The Dissent:**

Mr Justice Chan PJ dissented. He would have rejected the appellant's arguments on both the statutory construction issue and the constitutional issue and dismissed her appeal. On the statutory construction issue, he agreed that it was clearly the intention of the legislation to adopt the approach in Corbett v Corbett for the purpose of marriage and that "man" and "woman" in section 40 of the Marriage Ordinance and section 20(1)(d) of the Matrimonial Causes Ordinance mean a biological man and a biological woman. As a matter of statutory construction, these words are not capable of being given an extended meaning to cover a transsexual man or woman.

On the constitutional issue, Chan PJ held that the meaning contended for by the appellant of "man" and "woman" is different from the ordinary meaning of these words. It amounts to a radical change to the traditional concept of marriage. It also represents a departure from the domestic law as it was and still is which must have been intended to be the basis of Article 37 when it was first enacted. He held that the ordinary meanings of "man" and "woman" for the purpose of marriage refer respectively to a biological man and a biological woman capable of producing children. These words do not include a post-operative transsexual man and woman.

Chan PJ held that there is no evidence that in Hong Kong, these words have acquired any new contemporary meanings which are different from what is commonly understood by these words. He held that the meaning contended for by the appellant will have an extremely important effect on the concept of marriage. Marriage has been generally recognized as the basis of the family and the family is an essential unit of society. Although there are men and women who do not go through marriage, it is still regarded as an important social institution. It has a long history and in many countries, also a religious and cultural background.

Chan PJ held that there is no evidence that social attitudes in Hong Kong on the institution of marriage have changed to the extent that this concept of marriage has been abandoned or generally and substantially weakened. He held that when the Basic Law was drafted in the 1980s and promulgated in 1990, the meaning of marriage in Article 37 must have been informed by the state of the domestic legislation at the time. The right to marry under that article was clearly intended to refer to the right to marry of a man and woman as it was then understood. The case law and statute law have adopted the Corbett approach, i.e. applying only the biological criteria, in deciding whether a party to a marriage is a man or woman. That was the basis of the right to marry intended to be protected under Article 37 when it was drafted/adopted and promulgated.

Chan PJ held that while a constitutional provision can be given an updated meaning if the circumstances so require, there must be strong and compelling reasons for the Court now to depart from what has been generally understood to be the law on a matter as fundamental as the marriage institution which has its basis in the social attitudes of the community. A firm line has to be drawn between giving an updated interpretation to a constitutional provision to meet the needs of changing circumstances on the one hand and making a new policy on a social issue on the other. The latter is not the business of the court. For the former function, the court must be satisfied that there is sufficient evidence to show that the present circumstances in Hong Kong are such as to require the court to construe Article 37 differently from the law which formed the basis on which this article was drafted/adopted. In his view, in the absence of such evidence, the Court should not invoke its power of constitutional interpretation to make such a radical change.

Chan PJ held that the present position in Hong Kong is quite different from that in Europe and the UK when Goodwin was decided. While there was evidence of the changing attitudes in both Europe and the UK, he did not think there is sufficient evidence to show that the circumstances in Hong Kong are such as to justify the Court giving an interpretation to Article 37 to include transsexual men and women for the purpose of marriage. He held that there is no evidence showing that for the purpose of marriage, the ordinary meanings of man and woman in Hong Kong have changed to accommodate a transsexual man and woman. More importantly, there is no evidence that the social attitudes in Hong Kong towards the traditional concept of marriage and the marriage institution have fundamentally altered. Nor is there evidence on the degree of social acceptance of transsexualism.

Chan PJ held that giving recognition to the reassigned gender for the purpose of marriage involves a change of social policy. In his view, the court's power to give an updated interpretation is to react to changing circumstances and reflect changing social attitudes. The role of the court is to give effect to a change in an existing social policy, not to introduce any new social policy. The former is a judicial process but the latter is a matter for the democratic process. Social policy issues should not be decided by the court.

Chan PJ held that recognition of transsexuals for the purpose of marriage involves a major change in the law on the institution of marriage which calls for a comprehensive study and wide public consultation; it is only one aspect of the whole picture which needs to be investigated. He held that there is a strong case for a comprehensive review of the relevant legislation with a view to propose changes in the law concerning the problems facing transsexuals as soon as practicable.

**Mr Justice Bokhary NPJ's Concurring Judgment:**

Mr Justice Bokhary NPJ held that anyone who has been certified by an appropriate medical authority as someone whose sex has been reassigned by surgery is to be seen in the eyes of Hong Kong law as a person of the reassigned sex. W has been so certified as female. He held that the right to marry guaranteed by the constitution extends to the right of a post-operative transsexual to marry in the reassigned capacity. This means, without any need to rely on freedom from arbitrary or unlawful interference with privacy, that the legislation concerned would be unconstitutional unless the words of gender therein are read to include gender acquired by sex reassignment surgery. If those words can be so read, they should be so read. In his view, they can be so read. And, by way of remedial interpretation, he so read them.

Bokhary NPJ emphasized that one of the functions – perhaps by far the most important one – of constitutionally guaranteed human rights is to protect minorities. Why is there any need to guarantee a right to marry? After all, no society is likely to put impediments in the way of the majority entering into marriages as they like. The greatest and most urgent need for constitutional protection is apt to be found among those who form a minority, especially a misunderstood minority.

Bokhary NPJ also emphasized that a declaration of incompatibility under the Human Rights Act is by no means exactly the same thing as a striking-down order under an entrenched constitution. A declaration of incompatibility may well result in corrective legislation. Indeed, it may result in such legislation very quickly. But, unlike a striking-down order, it does not nullify the legislation against which it was made. Nor does it impose any legal duty to introduce or pass corrective legislation at all let alone quickly. By a declaration of incompatibility the judiciary alerts the political branches of government to legislation which cannot be given a reading compatible with Convention rights, but ultimately still leaves the matter in the hands of those branches of government. A striking-down order, on the other hand, is truly a course of last resort in the fullest sense. It is a more radical course than a remedial interpretation – even where a period of Koo suspension is granted to afford an opportunity for corrective legislation.

**Novel and Controversial Points:**

1. The Court's adoption of a "living instrument" approach to constitutional interpretation, taking account of medical advances and changed societal attitudes, represents a significant departure from the traditional approach of the Hong Kong courts.

2. The Court's rejection of the consensus argument – that the absence of a majority consensus should be a reason for denying recognition of minority rights – is a significant statement of constitutional principle.

3. The Court's willingness to apply a remedial interpretation to the statutory provisions, rather than simply striking them down, represents an important exercise of the Court's constitutional remedial powers.

4. The Court's recognition that the right to marry extends to post-operative transsexuals in their acquired gender represents a landmark development in Hong Kong's human rights jurisprudence.

5. The Court's acknowledgment that the Corbett criteria are outdated and inadequate, and its adoption of a multi-factorial approach to assessing sexual identity, represents a significant shift in the legal understanding of transsexualism.

6. The Court's decision to suspend the operation of the declarations for 12 months to allow the legislature to consider enacting comprehensive legislation represents a pragmatic approach to constitutional remedies.

7. The Court's recognition that the existing statutory provisions impair the "very essence" of the right to marry for post-operative transsexuals represents an important application of the proportionality principle in constitutional law.

## 4. Striking, Unusual or Remarkable Aspects of the Case

**The Description of the Transsexual Woman in Corbett:**

One of the most striking aspects of the case is the language used by Ormrod J in Corbett to describe the transsexual woman, April Ashley. The judgment contains passages that are remarkable for their lack of sensitivity and their dismissive tone. Ormrod J described the respondent's appearance as follows:

"Socially, by which I mean the manner in which the respondent is living in the community, she is living as, and passing as a woman, more or less successfully. Her outward appearance at first sight was convincingly feminine but on closer and longer examination in the witness box it was much less so. The voice, manner, gestures and attitudes became increasingly reminiscent of the accomplished female impersonator. The evidence of the medical inspectors and of the other doctors who had an opportunity during the trial of examining the respondent clinically is that the body in its post-operative condition looks more like a female than a male as a result of very skilful surgery. Professor Dewhurst, after this examination, put his opinion in these words: 'the pastiche of femininity was convincing.' That, in my judgment, is an accurate description of the respondent."

The Court of Final Appeal in the present case described this passage as suggesting "an element of artifice" and as betraying "a failure to recognize the fundamental importance and potency of the individual's psychological compulsion as a determinant of her sexual identity." The Court also noted that the term "pseudo-type of woman" was used by Nestadt J in the South African case of W v W, and that it would be "quite wrong" to exclude a transsexual person from the right to marry by characterising her in such terms.

This language is striking not only for its lack of sensitivity but also for what it reveals about the social and legal attitudes towards transsexuals in the 1970s. The fact that such language was used in a leading judgment of the English courts, and that it was not expressly disapproved until the present case, is a remarkable commentary on the slow progress of legal recognition of transsexual rights.

**The Suicide of Louise Chan and Sasha Moon:**

The judgment contains a reference to the tragic suicides of two transgender women in Hong Kong in 2004. Professor Robyn Emerton is quoted as follows:

"The plight of Hong Kong's transgender persons recently came to the fore after the suicide of Louise Chan, a young transgender woman, on 21 September 2004. Louise first came to the public's attention when she was stalked and 'outed' by the local media in 2003 resulting, amongst other things, in the loss of her job. Two days after Louise's death, another transgender woman, Sasha Moon, also committed suicide."

This passage is striking for its stark illustration of the human cost of social prejudice and the lack of legal recognition. The fact that two transgender women took their own lives within days of each other, following the "outing" of one by the media, is a tragic reminder of the real-world consequences of the legal and social issues at stake in this case. It is also a reminder that the law's recognition of transsexual persons is not merely a technical legal question but a matter of life and death for some of the most vulnerable members of society.

**The "Pastiche of Femininity" and the "Female Impersonator":**

The use of the terms "pastiche of femininity" and "female impersonator" in Corbett is striking for its dismissive and almost mocking tone. The Court of Final Appeal in the present case rightly criticized this language, noting that it "suggests an element of artifice" and "betrays a failure to recognize the fundamental importance and potency of the individual's psychological compulsion as a determinant of her sexual identity." The fact that such language was used in a leading judgment, and that it took over 40 years for it to be expressly disapproved, is a remarkable commentary on the slow progress of legal recognition of transsexual rights.

**The "Pseudo-Type of Woman":**

The use of the term "pseudo-type of woman" by Nestadt J in the South African case of W v W is similarly striking. The Court of Final Appeal in the present case held that it would be "quite wrong" to exclude a transsexual person from the right to marry by characterising her in such terms. The fact that such language was used in a common law jurisdiction, and that it was not expressly disapproved until the present case, is a remarkable commentary on the slow progress of legal recognition of transsexual rights.

**The "Intermediate Zone":**

The Court of Final Appeal quoted with approval Lord Nicholls' observation in Bellinger that recognizing an individual's change of gender is necessary to avoid "condemning post-operative transsexual people to live in what was aptly described by the European Court of Human Rights in the Goodwin case as an intermediate zone, not quite one gender or the other." This phrase is striking for its vivid description of the legal limbo in which post-operative transsexuals find themselves when the law refuses to recognize their acquired gender. It captures the human reality of the legal problem in a way that technical legal language cannot.

**The "Very Essence" of the Right to Marry:**

The Court's holding that the statutory provisions impair the "very essence" of W's right to marry is striking for its recognition that the right to marry is not merely a formal right but a substantive one that must be meaningful in practice. The Court's observation that "it is artificial to assert that post-operative transsexuals have not been deprived of the right to marry as, according to law, they remain able to marry a person of their former opposite sex" is a powerful statement of the principle that legal rights must be assessed in the light of real-world possibilities, not abstract formalities.

**The "Living Instrument" Doctrine:**

The Court's adoption of the "living instrument" approach to constitutional interpretation is striking for its recognition that the Basic Law and the Bill of Rights are not static documents but must be interpreted in the light of changing circumstances. The Court's observation that "it is one thing to have regard to such changes as a basis for accepting a more generous interpretation of a fundamental right and quite another to point to the absence of a majority consensus as a reason for denying recognition of minority rights" is a powerful statement of constitutional principle.

**The Rejection of the Consensus Argument:**

The Court's rejection of the consensus argument is striking for its recognition that fundamental rights are not subject to majority approval. The Court's citation of Murray CJ's observation that "the very application of a doctrine of consensus by a court required to adjudicate on fundamental rights begs important questions of legitimacy" is a powerful statement of the principle that courts must protect minority rights even in the face of majority opposition.

**The Suspension of the Declarations:**

The Court's decision to suspend the operation of the declarations for 12 months is striking for its pragmatic approach to constitutional remedies. The Court recognized that the legislature may potentially play a highly valuable and constructive role in making provision for certain legal consequences that flow from the ruling of unconstitutionality. The Court's willingness to await further submissions from the parties before finalising the Orders to be made, and its amenability to suspending the operation of such Orders for an appropriate period to give time for the enactment of legislation, represents a mature and considered approach to the exercise of constitutional jurisdiction.

**The Dissent of Chan PJ:**

The dissent of Chan PJ is striking for its emphasis on the need for judicial restraint in matters of social policy. Chan PJ's observation that "the role of the court is to give effect to a change in an existing social policy, not to introduce any new social policy" is a powerful statement of the principle of judicial restraint. His observation that "social policy issues should not be decided by the court" is a reminder of the limits of judicial power in a democratic society.

**The International Dimension:**

The case is striking for its international dimension. The Court received submissions from the International Commission of Jurists, and the judgment contains extensive references to the case-law of the European Court of Human Rights, the House of Lords, the Australian Family Court, the New Zealand Court of Appeal, and the South African courts. The Court's recognition that Hong Kong's position on marriage presently differs from the position adopted in many other countries, and its acknowledgment that in the Asia-Pacific region such marriages are permitted on the Mainland and in Canada, India, Singapore, Japan, South Korea, Indonesia, Australia and New Zealand, is a striking reminder of Hong Kong's place in the international community.

**The Human Cost:**

Perhaps the most striking aspect of the case is the human cost of the legal and social issues at stake. The judgment contains references to the suffering of transsexual persons, the "turmoil" they experience, and the tragic suicides of Louise Chan and Sasha Moon. The Court's recognition that "much suffering is involved for those afflicted with gender identity disorder" and that "non-recognition of their reassigned gender can cause them acute distress" is a powerful reminder that the law is not an abstract system of rules but a human institution that affects real people's lives in profound ways.

**The "Pastiche of Femininity" and the "Female Impersonator":**

The use of the terms "pastiche of femininity" and "female impersonator" in Corbett is striking for its dismissive and almost mocking tone. The Court of Final Appeal in the present case rightly criticized this language, noting that it "suggests an element of artifice" and "betrays a failure to recognize the fundamental importance and potency of the individual's psychological compulsion as a determinant of her sexual identity." The fact that such language was used in a leading judgment, and that it took over 40 years for it to be expressly disapproved, is a remarkable commentary on the slow progress of legal recognition of transsexual rights.

**The "Pseudo-Type of Woman":**

The use of the term "pseudo-type of woman" by Nestadt J in the South African case of W v W is similarly striking. The Court of Final Appeal in the present case held that it would be "quite wrong" to exclude a transsexual person from the right to marry by characterising her in such terms. The fact that such language was used in a common law jurisdiction, and that it was not expressly disapproved until the present case, is a remarkable commentary on the slow progress of legal recognition of transsexual rights.

**The "Intermediate Zone":**

The Court of Final Appeal quoted with approval Lord Nicholls' observation in Bellinger that recognizing an individual's change of gender is necessary to avoid "condemning post-operative transsexual people to live in what was aptly described by the European Court of Human Rights in the Goodwin case as an intermediate zone, not quite one gender or the other." This phrase is striking for its vivid description of the legal limbo in which post-operative transsexuals find themselves when the law refuses to recognize their acquired gender. It captures the human reality of the legal problem in a way that technical legal language cannot.

**The "Very Essence" of the Right to Marry:**

The Court's holding that the statutory provisions impair the "very essence" of W's right to marry is striking for its recognition that the right to marry is not merely a formal right but a substantive one that must be meaningful in practice. The Court's observation that "it is artificial to assert that post-operative transsexuals have not been deprived of the right to marry as, according to law, they remain able to marry a person of their former opposite sex" is a powerful statement of the principle that legal rights must be assessed in the light of real-world possibilities, not abstract formalities.

**The "Living Instrument" Doctrine:**

The Court's adoption of the "living instrument" approach to constitutional interpretation is striking for its recognition that the Basic Law and the Bill of Rights are not static documents but must be interpreted in the light of changing circumstances. The Court's observation that "it is one thing to have regard to such changes as a basis for accepting a more generous interpretation of a fundamental right and quite another to point to the absence of a majority consensus as a reason for denying recognition of minority rights" is a powerful statement of constitutional principle.

**The Rejection of the Consensus Argument:**

The Court's rejection of the consensus argument is striking for its recognition that fundamental rights are not subject to majority approval. The Court's citation of Murray CJ's observation that "the very application of a doctrine of consensus by a court required to adjudicate on fundamental rights begs important questions of legitimacy" is a powerful statement of the principle that courts must protect minority rights even in the face of majority opposition.

**The Suspension of the Declarations:**

The Court's decision to suspend the operation of the declarations for 12 months is striking for its pragmatic approach to constitutional remedies. The Court recognized that the legislature may potentially play a highly valuable and constructive role in making provision for certain legal consequences that flow from the ruling of unconstitutionality. The Court's willingness to await further submissions from the parties before finalising the Orders to be made, and its amenability to suspending the operation of such Orders for an appropriate period to give time for the enactment of legislation, represents a mature and considered approach to the exercise of constitutional jurisdiction.

**The Dissent of Chan PJ:**

The dissent of Chan PJ is striking for its emphasis on the need for judicial restraint in matters of social policy. Chan PJ's observation that "the role of the court is to give effect to a change in an existing social policy, not to introduce any new social policy" is a powerful statement of the principle of judicial restraint. His observation that "social policy issues should not be decided by the court" is a reminder of the limits of judicial power in a democratic society.

**The International Dimension:**

The case is striking for its international dimension. The Court received submissions from the International Commission of Jurists, and the judgment contains extensive references to the case-law of the European Court of Human Rights, the House of Lords, the Australian Family Court, the New Zealand Court of Appeal, and the South African courts. The Court's recognition that Hong Kong's position on marriage presently differs from the position adopted in many other countries, and its acknowledgment that in the Asia-Pacific region such marriages are permitted on the Mainland and in Canada, India, Singapore, Japan, South Korea, Indonesia, Australia and New Zealand, is a striking reminder of Hong Kong's place in the international community.

**The Human Cost:**

Perhaps the most striking aspect of the case is the human cost of the legal and social issues at stake. The judgment contains references to the suffering of transsexual persons, the "turmoil" they experience, and the tragic suicides of Louise Chan and Sasha Moon. The Court's recognition that "much suffering is involved for those afflicted with gender identity disorder" and that "non-recognition of their reassigned gender can cause them acute distress" is a powerful reminder that the law is not an abstract system of rules but a human institution that affects real people's lives in profound ways. [end of text]


